<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Nurpur State</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 CE – 1815 CE (approx.) · capital Nurpur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Pathania Rajput hill state whose Rajas served and defied the Mughal emperors, and whose capital Dhameri was renamed Nurpur in honour of Jahangir.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Pathania <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a> first ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pathankot">Pathankot</a> (Paithan) on the edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> plains, and tradition places their origins around 1000. In the 16th century they moved their seat into the hills at Dhameri, which was renamed Nurpur in the early 17th century after the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a>, Nur-ud-din.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nurpur's Rajas were prominent Mughal commanders. Raja Basu fortified Nurpur and served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a>, while his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagat-singh-of-nurpur">Jagat Singh</a> campaigned for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/islamic-emirate-of-afghanistan-2021-present">Afghanistan</a> before rebelling against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> in 1641–1642; after a long siege of his forts he was pardoned. The state lay on the route from the plains into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chamba-state">Chamba</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kangra-state">Kangra</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> annexed Nurpur to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> in 1815, and Raja Bir Singh died in 1846 while trying to recover it. The area passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/british-raj">British India</a> in 1846 and is now in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kangra-state">Kangra</a> district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nurpur-state.jpg" alt="Nurpur State" width="320" height="384"><figcaption>Image: Surveyed by Alexander Cunningham, drawn by Kalleemoodden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail_of_the_main,_continuous_tract_of_territory_of_Nurpur_from_a_map_of_the_various_Hill_States_of_the_Punjab_Hills_region,_copied_in_1852.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 CE – 1815 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nurpur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Basu, Jagat Singh, Bir Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kangri</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nurpur, Dhameri</dd></dl></aside></div>

